.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt MAKEWHATIS 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm makewhatis
.Nd index the manual pages
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl an
.Op Fl C Ar file
.Nm
.Op Fl an
.Ar dir ...
.Nm
.Op Fl n
.Fl d Ar dir
.Op Ar file ...
.Nm
.Op Fl n
.Fl u Ar dir
.Op Ar file ...
.Nm
.Fl t Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility writes the index that
.Xr apropos 1
searches and
.Xr man 1
finds pages by: the file
.Pa oxdoc.db
in each manual directory.
For every page in the directory's
.Pa man Ns Ar N
subdirectories and their architecture subdirectories, it records the
page's names, sections, architecture and one-line description, the values
of the
.Xr mdoc 7
macros listed in
.Xr apropos 1 ,
and every word of its text.
A file consisting of a
.Ic \&.so
request alone, or a hard link to another page, adds its name to that page.
.Pp
Without arguments,
.Nm
indexes every manual directory named in
.Pa /etc/man.conf ;
with
.Ar dir
arguments, those directories.
A new index replaces the old one only once it is complete.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Accepted for compatibility: all architectures are always indexed.
.It Fl C Ar file
Read the manual directories from
.Ar file
instead of
.Pa /etc/man.conf .
.It Fl d Ar dir Op Ar file ...
Index each
.Ar file
again in the existing index of
.Ar dir ,
adding it if it is new.
.It Fl n
Do everything but write the index.
.It Fl t Ar file ...
Check that each
.Ar file
can be indexed usefully: that it has a one-line description.
.It Fl u Ar dir Op Ar file ...
Remove each
.Ar file
from the index of
.Ar dir .
.El
.Pp
The index format is described in
.Pa MAN.md
of the OxideBSD documentation; a program finding an index of another
version ignores it and says so.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/man.conf -compact
.It Pa oxdoc.db
the index in each manual directory
.It Pa /etc/man.conf
the manual directories
.It Pa /usr/share/man/oxdoc.db
the index of the system's pages, built with the system
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr apropos 1 ,
.Xr man 1 ,
.Xr man.conf 5
.Sh HISTORY
This implementation was written for OxideBSD; its keys follow
.Xr mandoc 1 Ns 's
.Nm .
